{
  "name": "mac-derived-id",
  "integration": "ubiquiti-unifi-site-manager",
  "description": "REGRESSION LOCK for a MAC-derived foreign id that was allowed to drive merges. Both `devices[].id` and the orphan-console fallback `reportedState.hardware.mac` produce an id of the form unifi-site-manager:<ns>:device:<MAC with separators stripped>, and the assets carried `no-mac-break no-ip-break no-name-break` -- which leaves id-match and id-break ON, so an address-derived value could both drive and veto a merge. normalize_mac clears the locally administered bit for cross-source matching, so two distinct endpoints can fold onto one value: correct for an interface, wrong for identity. It escaped the no_mac_in_id invariant only because that check deliberately matches separator-bearing MACs, since a bare twelve-hex run cannot be told apart from a legitimate opaque id.\n\nThe id itself is kept -- an offline console imported under its host id and then under its device id the week it returns would be two assets for one appliance, which is worse -- so the fix is the one aruba-clearpass and forescout-counteract use for this identifier class: `no-id-match no-id-break`, with correlation falling back to the MAC, the address, and the name. The sibling ubiquiti-unifi-protect avoided the problem outright by keying on a Mongo ObjectId; the Site Manager API publishes no comparable non-MAC id.\n\nThree id shapes are asserted in one run, because the distinction is the whole point: a device row (MAC-derived), an orphan console with a hardware MAC (MAC-derived), and a self-hosted Network Server that has no MAC and therefore keeps its opaque host UUID. Only the third may still drive a merge, and _match.behavior is the only place that decision is observable.",
